{"id": 31, "ts": 1779908124.2131078, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "agent_3", "message": "I agree with Anna and Markus that Candidate\u202fC remains the strongest overall.\u202fWhile we still lack direct evidence of C\u2019s stress\u2011resilience and technical competence, the facts we do have\u2014100\u202f% operational reliability and the ability to make correct decisions quickly\u2014address the two most safety\u2011critical criteria for long\u2011distance flights.\u202fHis proven reliability and rapid, accurate decision\u2011making outweigh the communication difficulty, especially since the panel can mitigate that with targeted training.\nCandidate\u202fD shows some promising signs (takes responsibility seriously, solves problems extremely well, and responds adequately to unexpected events), but the only crew\u2011cooperation evidence we have is that he is regarded as arrogant and has weak leadership.\u202fThose traits pose a higher risk to cockpit harmony than C\u2019s egocentric reputation, and we have no concrete data on D\u2019s decision quality under pressure or attention to detail.\u202fTherefore, D\u2019s strengths do not compensate for the serious interpersonal concerns.\nGiven the current evidence, I keep my provisional vote for Candidate\u202fC, pending a brief plan to address his communication style during onboarding."}
